window.FULL_TESTS = [{"id":1,"minutes":20,"listening":"F1","language":[{"id":"FL1","title":"An Email to a Teacher","kind":"Email","text":"Dear Mr. Ortiz,\n\nI am writing to ask {1} I can hand in my history project on Monday instead of Friday. Last week I {2} sick with the flu and missed three days of school. I have already finished the research, but I still need {3} the poster. I promise it will be ready by Monday morning. Thank you for {4} my request.\n\nBest regards,\nDaniela","blanks":[{"o":["that","if","what","which"],"why":"\"Ask if\" introduces a yes/no question inside a sentence.","a":1},{"o":["am","have been","will be","was"],"why":"\"Last week\" is a finished time, so we use the past simple.","a":3},{"o":["to make","making","make","made"],"why":"\"Need\" is followed by \"to\" + verb when the subject does the action.","a":0},{"o":["consider","to consider","considering","considered"],"why":"After a preposition (\"for\") we use the -ing form.","a":2}]},{"id":"FL2","title":"Swimming Pool Notice","kind":"Notice","text":"The school swimming pool {1} closed from June 3 to June 10 for cleaning. During this time, swimming classes will take place at the city sports center, {2} is a ten-minute walk from the school. Students must bring their own towels {3} the sports center does not provide them. Teachers will walk with the classes, so no student should leave school {4}. Classes will return to the school pool on June 11, {5} the cleaning is finished on time.","blanks":[{"o":["has","being","will be","were"],"why":"The closing is in the future and the pool receives the action: \"will be closed\".","a":2},{"o":["which","who","where","what"],"why":"\", which\" adds information about a place used as the subject of \"is\".","a":0},{"o":["although","unless","so that","because"],"why":"The second part gives the reason for bringing towels.","a":3},{"o":["lonely","alone","only","along"],"why":"\"Alone\" means without other people. \"Lonely\" describes a sad feeling.","a":1},{"o":["as long as","even though","in spite of","instead of"],"why":"\"As long as\" means \"only if\": they return if the cleaning finishes on time.","a":0}]},{"id":"FL3","title":"A Message to a Friend","kind":"Text message","text":"Hi Tomás! I think I left my water bottle in your mom's car {1} we drove home from soccer practice yesterday. It's the blue one with stickers {2} it. Could you check for me? I {3} it since I was eight, so it's really special to me. If you find it, please bring it to school tomorrow. I {4} you a cookie from my lunch to say thank you!\nAna","blanks":[{"o":["during","since","until","when"],"why":"\"When\" + clause tells us the time. \"During\" must be followed by a noun, not a clause.","a":3},{"o":["in","on","at","over"],"why":"Stickers are stuck on a surface: \"stickers on it\".","a":1},{"o":["had","have","have had","am having"],"why":"\"Since I was eight\" shows a situation continuing until now: present perfect.","a":2},{"o":["will give","gave","giving","have given"],"why":"This is a promise about the future: \"will give\".","a":0}]},{"id":"FL4","title":"Class Trip to a Coffee Farm","kind":"Announcement","text":"Next Thursday, the seventh grade will visit a coffee farm near Chía. The bus will leave at 7:30 a.m., so please {1} late. At the farm, a guide will show us how coffee {2} from a small red fruit into the drink {3} many adults enjoy every morning. Students will also have the chance {4} their own coffee plant to take home. Lunch will be provided, but bring a hat, because it can be {5} sunny in the fields. We will return to school by 3:00 p.m. If you have {6} questions, please ask your homeroom teacher.","blanks":[{"o":["don't be","not be","be not","doesn't be"],"why":"A negative instruction uses \"don't\" + base verb: \"don't be late\".","a":0},{"o":["makes","is making","is made","made"],"why":"Coffee does not make anything; it is made by people, so we need the passive.","a":2},{"o":["what","that","who","where"],"why":"\"That\" begins a relative clause about a thing (the drink). \"What\" cannot follow a noun.","a":1},{"o":["planting","plant","planted","to plant"],"why":"\"The chance to do something\" uses the infinitive.","a":3},{"o":["much","too many","very","a lot"],"why":"\"Very\" goes before an adjective like \"sunny\".","a":2},{"o":["some of","any","much","a few of"],"why":"\"Any\" is used in \"if\" questions with plural nouns. \"Much\" is for uncountable nouns.","a":1}]},{"id":"FL5","title":"The Boy Who Fixes Bicycles","kind":"Part of a short story","text":"Every Saturday morning, Samuel opened the garage behind his grandfather's house and waited for his first customer. He was only twelve, but he {1} bicycles for almost two years. It all started when his own bike broke and his family could not afford {2} it repaired. His grandfather, {3} had been a mechanic, showed him how to fix the chain. Soon, neighbors started {4} their broken bikes to Samuel. He never asked for much money. \"If people can't pay,\" he said, \"they can bring me old parts {5}.\" Last month, a local newspaper wrote a story about him, and now he has {6} work that he needs his younger sister to help him. \"She's {7} than me at fixing brakes,\" he admits with a smile.","blanks":[{"o":["fixes","was fixed","has fixed","had been fixing"],"why":"The story is in the past, and the action continued up to that time: past perfect continuous.","a":3},{"o":["to get","getting","get","got"],"why":"\"Afford\" is followed by \"to\" + verb.","a":0},{"o":["which","whose","who","whom"],"why":"\"Who\" refers to a person and is the subject of \"had been\".","a":2},{"o":["to bringing","bringing","brought","bring"],"why":"\"Start\" can be followed by the -ing form: \"started bringing\".","a":1},{"o":["however","besides","otherwise","instead"],"why":"\"Instead\" means in place of money.","a":3},{"o":["so much","very much","too many","so many"],"why":"\"So much\" + uncountable noun + \"that\" shows a result. \"Work\" is uncountable.","a":0},{"o":["good","best","better","the better"],"why":"\"Than\" needs a comparative: \"better than\".","a":2}]},{"id":"FL6","title":"Why Do Cats Purr?","kind":"Magazine article","text":"Most people think that cats purr only {1} they are happy. It is true that many cats purr when someone strokes them. However, scientists have discovered that cats also purr when they are hurt, frightened or {2} ill. So what is the reason? One idea is that purring helps cats {3} themselves. A purr is a low sound that makes the cat's body vibrate gently. Some researchers believe that these vibrations may help bones and muscles heal {4}. This could explain {5} cats often purr after an injury. Mother cats also purr to their kittens. Newborn kittens cannot see or hear, {6} they can feel the vibrations and find their way to their mother. Although scientists have learned a lot, they still do not completely understand {7} cats make this sound. Perhaps the purr, {8} so many other things about cats, will always remain a little mysterious.","blanks":[{"o":["unless","when","before","until"],"why":"\"When\" means \"at the times that\": cats purr when they are happy.","a":1},{"o":["ever","also","too","even"],"why":"\"Even\" shows that something is surprising: they purr even when they are ill.","a":3},{"o":["calm","calming","to calming","calmed"],"why":"\"Help someone do something\": \"help\" can be followed by the base form.","a":0},{"o":["fast","more fastly","faster","fastest"],"why":"A comparison is meant (faster than without purring). \"Fastly\" is not a word.","a":2},{"o":["what","why","which","whose"],"why":"\"Explain why\" introduces a reason.","a":1},{"o":["so","or","because","but"],"why":"\"But\" shows contrast: they cannot see or hear, but they can feel.","a":3},{"o":["how","what","that","which"],"why":"\"Understand how\" means understand the way something happens.","a":0},{"o":["as","alike","like","likely"],"why":"\"Like\" + noun means \"similar to\".","a":2}]}],"reading":[{"id":"FR1","title":"Summer Reading Challenge","kind":"Notice","level":"A2-B1","text":["Chía Public Library is happy to announce its Summer Reading Challenge! From July 1 to August 15, students aged 10 to 15 can take part. Here is how it works.","1. Sign up at the front desk with an adult. You will receive a reading card.","2. Every time you finish a book, write the title on your card and tell a librarian one thing you liked about it. The librarian will then add a stamp to your card.","3. When your card has five stamps, you will receive a free bookmark. With ten stamps, you can choose a free book from our gift shelf.","4. Graphic novels and audiobooks count, but every book must have at least 100 pages.","Everyone who completes ten books will be invited to a pizza party on August 20. The student who reads the most books will have his or her photo displayed at the library entrance for one month."],"questions":[{"type":"Purpose","q":"What is the main purpose of the notice?","o":["To announce that the library has new books","To ask students to work at the library","To explain how to take part in a reading program","To invite families to a pizza restaurant"],"why":"The notice explains the steps and rules of the Summer Reading Challenge.","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"What must students do to get a stamp?","o":["Tell a librarian something they liked about a book","Write a short report about the book","Read the book inside the library","Bring an adult to the library each time"],"why":"Rule 2: write the title and tell a librarian one thing you liked.","a":0},{"type":"Detail","q":"What will a student receive after finishing five books?","o":["A free book","An invitation to a party","A photo at the entrance","A free bookmark"],"why":"Rule 3: five stamps = a free bookmark. The book comes with ten stamps.","a":3},{"type":"Inference","q":"Which book would NOT count for the challenge?","o":["A 150-page graphic novel","A 60-page comic book","A 120-page adventure novel","A 300-page biography"],"why":"Every book must have at least 100 pages, so a 60-page book does not count.","a":1},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"displayed\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to","o":["shown","printed","hidden","taken"],"why":"The photo will be put where people can see it: shown.","a":0}]},{"id":"FR2","title":"The Wrong Notebook","kind":"Narrative","level":"B1","text":["On Friday afternoon, Santiago was in such a hurry to catch his ride home that he grabbed the wrong notebook from the classroom shelf. He didn't notice until Saturday morning, when he opened it to do his math homework and found, instead of numbers, page after page of drawings.","They were comics. A small girl with a giant backpack traveled through a city where the buildings were made of books. On one page, she climbed a staircase of dictionaries; on another, she sailed across a lake of ink. Every drawing was full of tiny details. Santiago read the whole notebook twice.","On the last page, there was a name: Ana Ríos. Santiago was surprised. Ana had joined his class two months earlier, and she almost never spoke. She sat in the back row and kept her head down. Santiago realized that he had never once asked her a question.","On Monday, he found her by the lockers, looking worried. \"I think you have my notebook,\" she said quietly, \"and I think I have yours.\" They swapped. Before she could walk away, Santiago said, \"Your comics are amazing. The school newspaper needs someone to draw. You should talk to Mr. Ortiz.\" Ana's cheeks turned pink. \"You read it?\" she asked. \"Twice,\" he admitted.","Two weeks later, the first comic strip by Ana Ríos appeared on the back page of the school newspaper. Students crowded around the bulletin board to read it. In the corner of the last box, if you looked carefully, there was a tiny boy carrying the wrong notebook."],"questions":[{"type":"Detail","q":"How did Santiago find out that he had the wrong notebook?","o":["Ana called him on Saturday","He opened it to do his homework","His teacher told him on Friday","His friend saw it in his bag"],"why":"Paragraph 1: he noticed on Saturday when he opened it to do his math homework.","a":1},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"swapped\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["lost","signed","opened","exchanged"],"why":"Each one gave the other the right notebook.","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"Why was Santiago surprised when he saw the name on the last page?","o":["The drawings belonged to a quiet new classmate","Ana was in a higher grade","Ana already drew for the newspaper","He did not know anyone called Ana"],"why":"Paragraph 3: Ana had joined two months earlier and almost never spoke.","a":0},{"type":"Inference","q":"What does the sentence \"he had never once asked her a question\" suggest?","o":["He did not like talking in class","Ana never answered questions","He had not tried to get to know her","He was angry with her"],"why":"He realized he had never made an effort to talk to her.","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"What did Santiago suggest that Ana do?","o":["Show her comics to the whole class","Talk to Mr. Ortiz about drawing for the newspaper","Enter a drawing contest","Join the art club"],"why":"\"The school newspaper needs someone to draw. You should talk to Mr. Ortiz.\"","a":1},{"type":"Inference","q":"Why did Ana probably draw a tiny boy carrying the wrong notebook?","o":["Because Santiago asked her to","Because Mr. Ortiz wanted a boy in the comic","Because she had lost her notebook again","As a small, hidden thank-you to Santiago"],"why":"The boy refers to how Santiago found her comics and helped her get published.","a":3}]},{"id":"FR3","title":"Rivers in the Sky","kind":"Academic (science)","level":"B1-B2","text":["When people think of rivers, they usually imagine water flowing across the land. But in South America, some of the most important rivers flow through the sky. Scientists call them \"flying rivers.\"","These rivers begin in the Amazon rainforest. Every day, the rainforest's trees pull water from the soil through their roots. The water travels up to the leaves, where much of it escapes into the air as vapor. This process is called transpiration. A single large tree can release hundreds of liters of water in one day, and the Amazon has hundreds of billions of trees. Together, they send an enormous amount of water vapor into the atmosphere.","Winds then carry this moisture west across the continent. When the winds reach the Andes Mountains, the mountains act like a wall and turn the moist air toward the south. As the air moves, it forms clouds, and the water falls as rain on farms and cities far away from the forest. Scientists believe that a large share of the rain in parts of southern Brazil, Paraguay and Argentina begins as water released by Amazon trees.","This is why many researchers worry about cutting down the rainforest. Fewer trees means less water vapor, and less vapor means weaker flying rivers. Over time, this could lead to drier weather and smaller harvests in regions that depend on this rain. In other words, protecting the Amazon is not only about saving animals and plants. It is also about protecting the water supply of millions of people."],"questions":[{"type":"Main idea","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["How water from Amazon trees produces rain in other places","Why rivers in the Amazon are getting smaller","How farmers in Argentina water their crops","Why the Andes Mountains are so high"],"why":"The passage explains how vapor from Amazon trees travels and falls as rain far away.","a":0},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"escapes\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["runs away from danger","turns into ice","gets out","disappears forever"],"why":"The water leaves the leaves and goes into the air.","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"According to paragraph 2, what is transpiration?","o":["The movement of rivers across the land","The release of water vapor from leaves","The process of rain falling on trees","The growth of roots into the soil"],"why":"Water escapes from the leaves as vapor: \"This process is called transpiration.\"","a":1},{"type":"Reference","q":"The word \"they\" in paragraph 2 (\"Together, they send...\") refers to","o":["scientists","liters of water","the roots","the Amazon's trees"],"why":"All the trees together send water vapor into the atmosphere.","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"What do the Andes Mountains do to the moist air?","o":["They produce more water vapor","They stop clouds from forming","They turn it toward the south","They store it for farms"],"why":"Paragraph 3: the mountains \"turn the moist air toward the south.\"","a":2},{"type":"Inference","q":"What can be inferred about cutting down the Amazon rainforest?","o":["It could reduce rain in places far from the forest","It would make the Andes Mountains wetter","It would create more flying rivers","It would affect only the animals in the forest"],"why":"Fewer trees means weaker flying rivers and drier weather in regions that depend on this rain.","a":0},{"type":"Purpose","q":"Why does the author mention \"the water supply of millions of people\"?","o":["To explain how cities clean their water","To show that the rainforest matters to people who live far away","To suggest that people should drink rainwater","To compare the Amazon with other rivers"],"why":"The author wants readers to see that the forest affects people's water, not only nature.","a":1}]}]},{"id":2,"minutes":20,"listening":"F2","language":[{"id":"F2G1","title":"A Birthday Invitation","kind":"Email","text":"Hi Camila,\n\nI'm having a small party at my house on Saturday {1} my thirteenth birthday. It starts at four o'clock and finishes at eight. My mom is going to make her famous empanadas, and we {2} a movie in the garden after dinner. Could you let me know {3} you can come by Thursday? I really hope you can make it, because it {4} be the same without you!\n\nSee you soon,\nLucas","blanks":[{"o":["for celebrate","celebrating to","to celebrate","celebrated"],"why":"\"To\" + verb shows the purpose of the party.","a":2},{"o":["are going to watch","watched","have watched","watching"],"why":"\"Be going to\" talks about a future plan.","a":0},{"o":["what","that","which","whether"],"why":"\"Let me know whether\" introduces a yes/no question.","a":3},{"o":["isn't","won't","doesn't","didn't"],"why":"Only \"won't\" can be followed by the base verb \"be\" to talk about the future.","a":1}]},{"id":"F2G2","title":"Museum Rules","kind":"Notice","text":"Welcome to the Gold Museum. To protect our collection, visitors are asked to follow these rules. Bags larger than a school backpack {1} at the entrance. Photos are allowed, {2} flash cameras can damage some of the oldest objects. Please do not touch the glass cases, and keep a safe distance {3} the displays. Children under twelve must stay {4} an adult at all times. Free guided tours start every hour, {5} the first one beginning at 10:00 a.m.","blanks":[{"o":["must leave","must be left","must leaving","must to leave"],"why":"The bags do not leave anything; visitors leave them. We need the passive: \"must be left\".","a":1},{"o":["so","and","or","but"],"why":"\"But\" shows a contrast: photos are fine, but flash is a problem.","a":3},{"o":["from","of","to","at"],"why":"We say \"keep a distance from\" something.","a":0},{"o":["by","for","with","among"],"why":"\"Stay with an adult\" means remain together with one.","a":2},{"o":["with","and","which","when"],"why":"\"With\" + noun + -ing adds extra information: \"with the first one beginning at 10:00\".","a":0}]},{"id":"F2G3","title":"Help with Homework","kind":"Text message","text":"Hey Mateo, are you busy? I'm stuck on question five of the science homework. I {1} it three times, but I still don't understand it. Do you know {2} we have to draw the graph or just write the answer? If it's easier, we could do a video call later. I'll be home {3} six o'clock. Thanks, you're {4} best!\nValeria","blanks":[{"o":["have read","read have","am reading","was read"],"why":"\"Three times\" up to now uses the present perfect.","a":0},{"o":["that","what","which","if"],"why":"\"Do you know if...\" asks a yes/no question inside a sentence.","a":3},{"o":["since","by","during","for"],"why":"\"By six o'clock\" means no later than six.","a":1},{"o":["a","an","the","more"],"why":"Superlatives use \"the\": \"the best\".","a":2}]},{"id":"F2G4","title":"School Garden Day","kind":"Announcement","text":"This Friday is School Garden Day! All classes will spend the last two periods outside, {1} vegetables and flowers in the new garden beside the soccer field. Please wear old clothes and shoes {2} can get dirty. Gloves and tools will be provided, but students who have their own gloves are welcome {3} them. The seventh grade will be in charge of {4} the tomato plants, and the eighth grade will build a small compost box. If it rains, the event {5} until the following Friday. We hope that, by the end of the year, the garden will produce {6} vegetables to use in our school cafeteria.","blanks":[{"o":["to planting","plant","planted","planting"],"why":"\"Spend time doing something\": the -ing form.","a":3},{"o":["what","that","who","whose"],"why":"\"That\" begins a relative clause about things (shoes).","a":1},{"o":["bringing","bring","to bring","brought"],"why":"\"Be welcome to do something\" uses the infinitive.","a":2},{"o":["watering","water","to water","watered"],"why":"After a preposition (\"of\") we use the -ing form.","a":0},{"o":["will postpone","postpones","has postponed","will be postponed"],"why":"The event does not postpone anything; it is postponed. We need the passive.","a":3},{"o":["too","so","enough","very"],"why":"\"Enough\" + noun means as many as needed.","a":2}]},{"id":"F2G5","title":"The Spelling Bee","kind":"Part of a short story","text":"Isabel stood on the stage, her hands shaking. Only two students were left in the school spelling bee, and the next word {1} decide the winner. \"Your word is 'necessary,'\" the judge said. Isabel closed her eyes. She {2} this word with her grandfather the night before, but now her mind was empty. \"N-E-C-C-E-S-S-A-R-Y,\" she said slowly. The bell rang. She {3} made a mistake. The other girl, {4} name was Paula, spelled the word correctly and won the trophy. Isabel felt terrible, but as she walked off the stage, Paula ran after her. \"You were amazing,\" Paula said. \"I was so nervous that I could {5} breathe.\" Isabel smiled for the first time that day. The two girls started {6} together after school, and by the next year they were best friends. \"Losing that word,\" Isabel said later, \"was {7} thing that ever happened to me.\"","blanks":[{"o":["will","would","was","has"],"why":"The story is in the past, so the future in the past is \"would\".","a":1},{"o":["has practiced","practices","had practiced","was practiced"],"why":"An action before another past action (\"the night before\") uses the past perfect.","a":2},{"o":["had","has","was","did"],"why":"\"Had made\" (past perfect): the mistake happened before the bell rang.","a":0},{"o":["who","which","that","whose"],"why":"\"Whose\" shows possession: the girl's name.","a":3},{"o":["hard","hardly","harder","hardest"],"why":"\"Hardly\" means almost not: she could almost not breathe.","a":1},{"o":["to studying","studied","study","studying"],"why":"\"Start\" can be followed by the -ing form.","a":3},{"o":["the best","the better","best","better"],"why":"\"The best thing that ever happened\" is a superlative and needs \"the\".","a":0}]},{"id":"F2G6","title":"Why Do We Yawn?","kind":"Magazine article","text":"Everybody yawns, from babies {1} grandparents. Even many animals, such as dogs, cats and lions, yawn. But scientists still do not agree on {2} we do it. For a long time, people believed that we yawn because our bodies need more oxygen. However, experiments have shown that breathing extra oxygen does not make people yawn {3}. A newer idea is that yawning helps {4} the brain. When we yawn, we take in a large amount of air, which may lower the temperature of the blood going to our heads. Yawning is also contagious. If you see someone yawn, you are much more likely to yawn {5}. Some researchers think this is connected to empathy, the ability to share and understand the feelings {6} other people. Interestingly, young children do not usually \"catch\" yawns {7} they are about four or five years old. So the next time you yawn in class, it might not mean you are bored. Your brain may {8} be trying to cool down!","blanks":[{"o":["from","with","to","by"],"why":"\"From ... to ...\" shows a range.","a":2},{"o":["why","what","which","who"],"why":"\"Agree on why\" introduces a reason.","a":0},{"o":["fewer","least","little","less"],"why":"\"Yawn less\" compares how much; \"fewer\" is used with plural nouns.","a":3},{"o":["cooling to","cool","cools","cooled"],"why":"\"Help\" can be followed by the base form: \"helps cool\".","a":1},{"o":["either","also","too","neither"],"why":"\"Too\" at the end of a positive sentence means \"as well\".","a":2},{"o":["of","from","at","by"],"why":"\"The feelings of other people\": \"of\" shows whose feelings.","a":0},{"o":["since","until","while","unless"],"why":"\"Until\" means up to the time when.","a":1},{"o":["simple","simplest","simplicity","simply"],"why":"An adverb (\"simply\") describes the verb \"be trying\".","a":3}]}],"reading":[{"id":"F2R1","title":"Cafeteria News: New Lunch Cards","kind":"Notice","level":"A2-B1","text":["Starting on March 1, the school cafeteria will use a new lunch card system. Instead of paying with cash, every student will receive a plastic card with his or her name and photo.","Parents can add money to the card online or at the school office. Each time a student buys lunch, the cost will be taken from the card automatically. Students can check how much money is left by touching the card to the screen next to the cash register.","If a card is lost, students should report it to the office immediately. A new card costs 5,000 pesos, and any money on the old card will be moved to the new one.","The new system will make the lunch line much faster. Last year, students waited an average of twelve minutes to buy lunch. With the cards, we expect the wait to be less than five minutes."],"questions":[{"type":"Main idea","q":"What is the notice mainly about?","o":["A change in the cafeteria menu","A new way to pay for lunch","New rules for behavior in the lunch line","A price increase for school lunches"],"why":"The notice explains the new lunch card system that replaces cash.","a":1},{"type":"Detail","q":"How can parents add money to the card?","o":["At the cash register","By sending cash with the student","At a bank near the school","Online or at the school office"],"why":"Paragraph 2: \"online or at the school office.\"","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"What happens to the money on a lost card?","o":["It is moved to the new card","It is lost forever","It is returned to the parents","It pays for the new card"],"why":"Paragraph 3: \"any money on the old card will be moved to the new one.\"","a":0},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"automatically\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["very slowly","only once a week","without anyone having to do anything","with the help of a teacher"],"why":"The cost is taken from the card by the system itself.","a":2},{"type":"Inference","q":"Why is the school starting the new system?","o":["To make students wait less time for lunch","To sell more food","To stop students from eating lunch at school","To save money on plastic"],"why":"Paragraph 4: the wait should drop from twelve minutes to less than five.","a":0}]},{"id":"F2R2","title":"The New Player","kind":"Narrative","level":"B1","text":["When Kenji arrived at his new school in Medellín, he did not speak much Spanish. He had moved from Japan with his parents only two weeks earlier, and everything felt strange: the food, the music, even the way people greeted each other with a kiss on the cheek.","At lunch, he usually sat alone and read a book. One day, he noticed a group of students playing chess at a table near the window. He moved closer to watch. A tall boy named Andrés looked up and pointed at the empty chair. Kenji sat down.","They could not talk much, but they did not need to. Chess has its own language. Kenji won the first game in fifteen minutes. Andrés laughed, shook his hand and set up the pieces again. By the end of lunch, a small crowd had gathered around the table.","The next week, Andrés invited Kenji to join the school chess club. In return, Kenji offered to teach the club some strategies he had learned from his grandfather in Osaka. Andrés helped him with his Spanish homework.","By the end of the year, Kenji was the captain of the chess team, and he could tell jokes in Spanish. \"I came here thinking I had no friends,\" he said in his speech at the end-of-year party. \"But I had thirty-two friends waiting for me all along: the pieces on a chessboard.\""],"questions":[{"type":"Detail","q":"Why did everything feel strange to Kenji?","o":["He had never been to school before","He did not like his new house","He had just moved from another country","His parents did not speak Spanish"],"why":"Paragraph 1: he had moved from Japan two weeks earlier.","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"What did Kenji usually do at lunch before he met Andrés?","o":["He read a book alone","He played chess with friends","He studied Spanish with a teacher","He called his parents"],"why":"Paragraph 2: \"he usually sat alone and read a book.\"","a":0},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"gathered\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["gone away","become quiet","started to play","come together"],"why":"A small crowd came together around the table to watch.","a":3},{"type":"Inference","q":"What does the author mean by \"Chess has its own language\"?","o":["Chess rules are written in Japanese","People can understand each other through the game without words","Chess players speak a special language","Kenji learned Spanish by playing chess"],"why":"They could not talk much, but the game let them communicate.","a":1},{"type":"Detail","q":"How did Kenji help the chess club?","o":["He helped members with their Spanish homework","He bought new chess sets","He organized the end-of-year party","He taught strategies he learned from his grandfather"],"why":"Paragraph 4: he offered to teach strategies from his grandfather in Osaka.","a":3},{"type":"Inference","q":"What does Kenji mean by \"thirty-two friends\"?","o":["He had thirty-two classmates","Chess was what helped him find friends","The chess club had thirty-two members","He won thirty-two games that year"],"why":"A chessboard has thirty-two pieces; the game connected him to people.","a":1}]},{"id":"F2R3","title":"The Farmers of the Forest","kind":"Academic (science)","level":"B1-B2","text":["Humans started farming about ten thousand years ago. But a group of insects had already been doing it for millions of years. Leafcutter ants, which live in the forests of Central and South America, including Colombia, grow their own food.","Walk through a forest where they live, and you may see a long line of ants, each carrying a piece of leaf much bigger than its own body. An ant can carry a load many times its own weight. But surprisingly, the ants do not eat these leaves. Instead, they take them deep underground into their nest.","Inside the nest, smaller ants chew the leaves into a soft paste. On this paste, they grow a special fungus, a little like a mushroom. The fungus is the ants' real food. The ants take care of their \"garden\" carefully: they remove any harmful mold, and they never let the fungus dry out. In return, the fungus feeds the whole colony, which can contain millions of ants.","Each colony begins with a single queen. When she flies away to start a new nest, she carries a small piece of fungus in her mouth to plant her first garden. In the Colombian department of Santander, these large queens are known as hormigas culonas, and people have roasted and eaten them as a snack for hundreds of years."],"questions":[{"type":"Main idea","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["Why ants live underground","How people cook ants in Santander","Why ants are stronger than humans","How leafcutter ants grow their own food"],"why":"The passage explains how the ants use leaves to grow the fungus they eat.","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"According to paragraph 1, when did leafcutter ants start farming?","o":["About ten thousand years ago","Millions of years before humans did","At the same time as humans","In the last hundred years"],"why":"They \"had already been doing it for millions of years\" when humans started.","a":1},{"type":"Detail","q":"What do the ants do with the leaves?","o":["They use them to grow fungus","They eat them right away","They build the walls of their nest with them","They feed them to the queen"],"why":"Paragraph 3: they chew the leaves into a paste and grow fungus on it.","a":0},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"load\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["a kind of food","a type of leaf","something that is carried","a long line"],"why":"The ant carries a load many times its own weight.","a":2},{"type":"Reference","q":"The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 (\"they remove any harmful mold\") refers to","o":["the leaves","the ants","the mushrooms","the humans"],"why":"The ants take care of their garden by removing mold.","a":1},{"type":"Detail","q":"What does a new queen take with her when she starts a nest?","o":["Leaves from the old nest","A group of worker ants","Food for the winter","A small piece of fungus"],"why":"\"She carries a small piece of fungus in her mouth to plant her first garden.\"","a":3},{"type":"Purpose","q":"Why does the author mention hormigas culonas?","o":["To show a connection between these ants and people in Colombia","To explain how the ants find food","To warn people not to eat ants","To describe how queens build nests"],"why":"It links the ants to a local Colombian tradition.","a":0}]}]},{"id":3,"minutes":20,"listening":"F3","language":[{"id":"F3G1","title":"A Letter to a Pen Pal","kind":"Letter","text":"Dear Emma,\n\nThank you for your last letter. I loved {1} about your trip to London! Here in Chía, the weather {2} very rainy lately, so I have been spending a lot of time indoors. I started learning the guitar last month, and I can already play three songs. My teacher says I learn {3} than most of his students. Next time, could you send me a photo of your school? I'd like to see {4} it looks like.\n\nYour friend,\nDaniel","blanks":[{"o":["to reading","read","reads","reading"],"why":"\"Love\" can be followed by the -ing form: \"loved reading\".","a":3},{"o":["has been","is been","was being","have been"],"why":"\"Lately\" goes with the present perfect, and \"the weather\" is singular.","a":0},{"o":["more fast","fastest","faster","the fastest"],"why":"\"Than\" needs a comparative. \"Fast\" becomes \"faster\".","a":2},{"o":["how","what","which","that"],"why":"We say \"see what it looks like\". \"How it looks like\" is not correct.","a":1}]},{"id":"F3G2","title":"Science Fair Rules","kind":"Notice","text":"The annual science fair will take place on May 15 in the school gym. All projects must be registered {1} April 30. Students may work alone or in pairs, but groups of three or more {2}. Every project must include a poster and a short report {3} the question, the experiment and the results. Projects {4} use fire or dangerous chemicals will not be accepted. Prizes will be given to the three best projects, and the winners will represent our school at the city competition, {5} will be held in June.","blanks":[{"o":["until","by","since","during"],"why":"\"By April 30\" means no later than that date.","a":1},{"o":["are not allowed","do not allow","not allowed","are not allowing"],"why":"Groups receive the action, so we need the passive \"are not allowed\".","a":0},{"o":["explains","explained by","to explaining","explaining"],"why":"\"A report explaining...\" = a report that explains.","a":3},{"o":["what","who","that","where"],"why":"\"That\" begins a relative clause about things (projects).","a":2},{"o":["which","where","what","when"],"why":"\", which\" refers to the city competition and is the subject of \"will be held\".","a":0}]},{"id":"F3G3","title":"Running Late","kind":"Text message","text":"Hi Mom! The bus is stuck in traffic, so I'll probably be about twenty minutes late. There was an accident on the highway, but don't worry, nobody {1} hurt. I'm sitting next to Sara, {2} lives near us, so we'll walk home together from the bus stop. Can you leave the key {3} the flowerpot? I forgot mine this morning. I'll text you again {4} I get off the bus.\nLove, Ana","blanks":[{"o":["were","is being","was","has"],"why":"\"Nobody\" is singular, and the accident is in the past: \"nobody was hurt\".","a":2},{"o":["which","who","whose","whom"],"why":"\"Who\" refers to a person and is the subject of \"lives\".","a":1},{"o":["between","among","through","under"],"why":"The key goes below the flowerpot: \"under\".","a":3},{"o":["as soon as","as long as","so that","even if"],"why":"\"As soon as\" means immediately when.","a":0}]},{"id":"F3G4","title":"Talent Show Auditions","kind":"Announcement","text":"Do you sing, dance, play an instrument or tell jokes? Then the school talent show is looking {1} you! Auditions will be held next Monday and Tuesday in the music room from 3:00 to 5:00 p.m. Each act can be {2} longer than three minutes. You can perform alone or with a group of up to five people. If you need a piano or a microphone, please tell Mr. Hale in advance so he can {3} them ready. Students who are chosen will practice every Thursday {4} the night of the show. The show itself will be on June 2, and tickets {5} at the school office. All the money raised will be used to buy {6} instruments for the music department.","blanks":[{"o":["for","at","after","to"],"why":"\"Look for\" means search for.","a":0},{"o":["not","none","nothing","no"],"why":"\"No longer than\" means not more than.","a":3},{"o":["getting","to get","get","got"],"why":"After \"can\" we use the base form.","a":2},{"o":["by","until","since","while"],"why":"\"Until\" shows that the practice continues up to that night.","a":1},{"o":["will sell them","are sold by","have sold","will be sold"],"why":"Tickets receive the action: the passive \"will be sold\".","a":3},{"o":["new","news","newly","newer than"],"why":"An adjective (\"new\") describes the noun \"instruments\".","a":0}]},{"id":"F3G5","title":"Grandma's Recipe","kind":"Part of a short story","text":"Every December, my grandmother made buñuelos for the whole family. Nobody else knew the recipe; she said she kept it in her head. When she got sick last year, I was worried that the recipe {1} forever. So one afternoon, I sat beside her bed with a notebook and asked her to teach me. \"You can't learn it from a notebook,\" she laughed. \"You have to {2} it with your hands.\" The next day, she sat in the kitchen and told me what to do, step by step. I mixed the cheese and the flour {3} the dough felt soft. My first buñuelos were {4} hard that my brother joked he could play soccer with them. But I kept trying. By the fourth batch, they were round, golden and light. My grandmother tasted one and said nothing for a long time. Then she smiled. \"Now,\" she said, \"the recipe has two homes.\" This December, I {5} the buñuelos for the first time. Grandma is better now, and she will sit next to me, {6} sure I don't forget anything. I'm not nervous, because I know the recipe is not only in my notebook. It's in my hands, {7}.","blanks":[{"o":["will be lost","was losing","would be lost","has lost"],"why":"A future idea seen from the past uses \"would\", and the recipe receives the action: \"would be lost\".","a":2},{"o":["feeling","feel","to feel","felt"],"why":"After \"have to\" we use the base form.","a":1},{"o":["since","unless","although","until"],"why":"\"Until\" means up to the moment when the dough felt soft.","a":3},{"o":["so","such","too","very"],"why":"\"So\" + adjective + \"that\" shows a result.","a":0},{"o":["made","have made","am going to make","was making"],"why":"This December is in the future, so we use \"be going to\".","a":2},{"o":["makes","making","to making","made"],"why":"The -ing form adds a second action happening at the same time.","a":1},{"o":["too","either","neither","also"],"why":"\"Too\" at the end of a positive sentence means \"as well\".","a":0}]},{"id":"F3G6","title":"Why Do Leaves Change Color?","kind":"Magazine article","text":"In many parts of the world, autumn turns forests into a sea of red, orange and yellow. But {1} do leaves change color? The answer has to do with a substance called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is {2} makes leaves green, and it helps trees turn sunlight into food. In spring and summer, leaves are full of chlorophyll. As autumn arrives, the days become shorter and colder, and trees start {3} for winter. They stop making chlorophyll, and the green color slowly disappears. When it is gone, other colors that {4} in the leaf all along, such as yellow and orange, finally become visible. Red is different. Some trees produce red substances only in autumn, especially when the days are sunny and the nights are cool. This is why the colors are brighter in some years {5} in others. In Colombia, most trees do not change color like this, {6} the country is close to the equator and does not have four seasons. Instead, many trees here lose {7} leaves during the dry season. So if you want to see the famous autumn colors, you will have to travel {8} north or far south.","blanks":[{"o":["what","which","where","why"],"why":"The article goes on to give the reason, so the question is \"why\".","a":3},{"o":["which","what","that","who"],"why":"\"What\" means \"the thing that\": chlorophyll is the thing that makes leaves green.","a":1},{"o":["to preparing","prepare","preparing","prepared"],"why":"\"Start\" can be followed by the -ing form.","a":2},{"o":["have been","has been","are being","were be"],"why":"\"All along\" (from the start until now) uses the present perfect; \"colors\" is plural.","a":0},{"o":["then","as","that","than"],"why":"\"Brighter ... than\" is a comparison.","a":3},{"o":["although","unless","because","so"],"why":"The second part gives the reason.","a":2},{"o":["their","its","there","they're"],"why":"\"Their\" shows that the leaves belong to the trees (plural).","a":0},{"o":["much","far","very","too"],"why":"\"Far north\" means a long way to the north.","a":1}]}],"reading":[{"id":"F3R1","title":"The New Student Bus Card","kind":"Notice","level":"A2-B1","text":["Students between 10 and 18 years old can now travel on all city buses for half price with the new Student Bus Card. The card works on every route, Monday to Saturday.","How to apply: Fill in the form on the city transport website, and upload a recent photo and a letter from your school showing that you are a student. Cards are ready about ten days after you apply. You must pick up your card in person at the Central Station office and show an ID.","Rules: The card has your name and photo and can only be used by you. If someone else uses your card, it will be cancelled. You can add money to the card at any station or in the city transport app. On Sundays and public holidays, the student price does not apply, and you will pay the normal price.","Lost your card? Report it immediately in the app, so no one else can use your money. A new card costs 8,000 pesos."],"questions":[{"type":"Purpose","q":"What is the main purpose of the notice?","o":["To explain how students can get a cheaper bus card","To announce new bus routes","To ask students to stop using buses","To sell tickets for a school trip"],"why":"The notice explains the half-price Student Bus Card, how to apply and the rules.","a":0},{"type":"Detail","q":"What must students upload with the form?","o":["Their parents' ID","Money for their first trip","A photo and a letter from their school","A copy of their report card"],"why":"\"Upload a recent photo and a letter from your school.\"","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"Where can students get their card?","o":["At their school","At the Central Station office","By mail","In the app"],"why":"\"You must pick up your card in person at the Central Station office.\"","a":1},{"type":"Inference","q":"How much will a student pay for a bus ride on a Sunday?","o":["Half price","Nothing","Double the normal price","The normal price"],"why":"\"On Sundays and public holidays, the student price does not apply.\"","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"What should a student do if the card is lost?","o":["Wait ten days","Report it in the app right away","Borrow a friend's card","Buy a new card at school"],"why":"\"Report it immediately in the app, so no one else can use your money.\"","a":1}]},{"id":"F3R2","title":"The Broken Telescope","kind":"Narrative","level":"B1","text":["For his twelfth birthday, Tomás received a telescope from his uncle, who worked at the planetarium in Bogotá. It was old and heavy, but Tomás loved it more than anything he owned. Every clear night, he carried it up to the roof of his building to look at the Moon and the planets.","One evening in March, his little sister Lina wanted to see Jupiter. She grabbed the telescope before Tomás could stop her, and it fell to the floor with a terrible crack. One of the lenses was broken. Lina burst into tears, and Tomás was so angry that he couldn't speak.","That night, he lay awake thinking. He remembered how excited Lina had been, and how she had asked him every day for a week when she could look through the telescope. He realized that she hadn't wanted to break it; she had wanted to share the thing he loved.","The next morning, Tomás called his uncle. \"Can lenses be replaced?\" he asked. His uncle laughed. \"Old telescopes are like old friends,\" he said. \"They can always be fixed. Bring it on Saturday, and bring your sister, too.\"","That Saturday, Tomás and Lina spent the whole afternoon at the planetarium, learning how lenses work and helping their uncle put in a new one. That night, the first thing they looked at together was Jupiter. Lina counted four tiny moons around it. \"You can borrow the telescope whenever you want,\" Tomás told her. \"Just ask me first.\""],"questions":[{"type":"Detail","q":"Who gave Tomás the telescope?","o":["His parents","His sister","A teacher at school","His uncle"],"why":"Paragraph 1: \"Tomás received a telescope from his uncle.\"","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"How was the telescope broken?","o":["Lina dropped it","Tomás dropped it on the roof","It fell from a window","His uncle sat on it"],"why":"Lina grabbed it and it fell to the floor.","a":0},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The phrase \"burst into tears\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["started laughing","ran away quickly","suddenly started crying","became very angry"],"why":"Lina suddenly began to cry after the telescope broke.","a":2},{"type":"Inference","q":"What did Tomás realize during the night?","o":["The telescope was too old to use","Lina only wanted to share something he loved","His uncle would be angry","Jupiter could not be seen in March"],"why":"Paragraph 3: \"she had wanted to share the thing he loved.\"","a":1},{"type":"Inference","q":"What does the uncle mean when he says \"Old telescopes are like old friends\"?","o":["Problems with them can be fixed","They are better than new ones","They should be given to friends","They are very expensive"],"why":"He adds, \"They can always be fixed.\"","a":0},{"type":"Detail","q":"What did Tomás and Lina do at the planetarium?","o":["They bought a new telescope","They watched a movie about Jupiter","They helped put in a new lens","They gave a talk about the Moon"],"why":"Paragraph 5: they helped their uncle put in a new lens.","a":2}]},{"id":"F3R3","title":"The First Vaccine","kind":"Academic (history of science)","level":"B1-B2","text":["For centuries, smallpox was one of the most feared diseases in the world. It caused fever and painful spots all over the body, and it killed about three out of every ten people who caught it. Those who survived were often left with deep scars.","In the 1790s, an English doctor named Edward Jenner noticed something interesting. Milkmaids, the women who milked cows on farms, often caught a mild disease called cowpox from the animals. Cowpox caused only a few spots and went away quickly. And strangely, milkmaids who had had cowpox almost never caught smallpox.","In 1796, Jenner decided to test his idea. He took material from a cowpox spot on the hand of a milkmaid named Sarah Nelmes and put it into a small cut on the arm of an eight-year-old boy, James Phipps. The boy became slightly ill but recovered in a few days. Weeks later, Jenner exposed him to smallpox. James did not get sick. His body had learned to protect itself.","Jenner called his method \"vaccination,\" from vacca, the Latin word for cow. The idea spread around the world. Over the next two centuries, vaccination programs became so successful that in 1980, the World Health Organization announced that smallpox had disappeared completely. It was the first human disease ever to be eliminated."],"questions":[{"type":"Main idea","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["Why milkmaids worked on farms","How the first vaccine was developed and what it achieved","How cows catch diseases","Why smallpox causes scars"],"why":"The passage tells the story of Jenner's vaccine and how it ended smallpox.","a":1},{"type":"Detail","q":"What did Jenner notice about milkmaids?","o":["They were often sick with smallpox","They never caught any diseases","They had deep scars on their hands","Those who had had cowpox rarely caught smallpox"],"why":"Paragraph 2: \"milkmaids who had had cowpox almost never caught smallpox.\"","a":3},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"mild\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["not serious","very dangerous","long-lasting","common"],"why":"Cowpox caused only a few spots and went away quickly.","a":0},{"type":"Detail","q":"What happened when James Phipps was exposed to smallpox?","o":["He became very sick but recovered","He caught cowpox again","He did not get sick","He died a few weeks later"],"why":"Paragraph 3: \"James did not get sick.\"","a":2},{"type":"Reference","q":"The word \"His\" in paragraph 3 (\"His body had learned...\") refers to","o":["Edward Jenner","the cow","a doctor in London","James Phipps"],"why":"It was the boy's body that had learned to protect itself.","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"Where does the word \"vaccination\" come from?","o":["The name of a milkmaid","The Latin word for cow","The name of Jenner's town","The Latin word for disease"],"why":"Paragraph 4: \"from vacca, the Latin word for cow.\"","a":1},{"type":"Inference","q":"What can be inferred about smallpox today?","o":["People no longer catch it","It is still common on farms","It can be cured with cowpox","Scientists are still looking for a vaccine"],"why":"In 1980 it was announced that smallpox had disappeared completely.","a":0}]}]},{"id":4,"minutes":20,"listening":"F4","language":[{"id":"F4G1","title":"An Email to a Coach","kind":"Email","text":"Dear Coach Silva,\n\nI'm sorry, but I won't be able to come to practice on Wednesday. My family is traveling to Cartagena for my cousin's wedding, and we {1} until Sunday night. I know the tournament is next week, so I {2} extra at home. Could you tell me {3} drills I should practice? I don't want my team {4} because of me.\n\nThank you,\nMartín","blanks":[{"o":["wasn't back","won't be back","haven't been back","aren't being back"],"why":"A future fact: \"won't be back until Sunday night\".","a":1},{"o":["trained","have trained","will train","was training"],"why":"This is a decision about the future: \"will train\".","a":2},{"o":["which","who","whose","whom"],"why":"\"Which drills\" asks about things. \"Who\" and \"whom\" are for people.","a":0},{"o":["losing","lose","lost","to lose"],"why":"\"Want someone to do something\" uses the infinitive.","a":3}]},{"id":"F4G2","title":"Zoo Visitor Rules","kind":"Notice","text":"Thank you for visiting the Santa Cruz Zoo. Please read these rules before you begin your visit. Feeding the animals is strictly {1}, because human food can make them sick. Stay on the marked paths at all times, and do not climb {2} the fences. In the Butterfly House, please move slowly and quietly {3} you don't frighten the butterflies. Drones and loud music are not permitted anywhere in the zoo. The zoo closes at 5:00 p.m., but the last tickets are sold an hour {4}. If you see an animal {5} seems sick or hurt, please tell a member of staff immediately.","blanks":[{"o":["forbid","forbidding","forbids","forbidden"],"why":"\"Is forbidden\" is passive: feeding is not allowed.","a":3},{"o":["over","above","across from","up to"],"why":"\"Climb over\" means go from one side to the other by climbing.","a":0},{"o":["in case","so that","even if","as if"],"why":"\"So that\" gives the purpose: to avoid frightening them.","a":1},{"o":["more early","earliest","earlier","the earlier"],"why":"\"An hour earlier\" = an hour before 5:00. \"More early\" is not correct.","a":2},{"o":["what","that","whose","where"],"why":"\"That\" begins a relative clause about the animal and is the subject of \"seems\".","a":1}]},{"id":"F4G3","title":"Movie Night","kind":"Text message","text":"Hi everyone! Movie night is at my place on Friday. My dad said we can use the big TV in the living room, {1} we clean up afterward. Bring a blanket if you want, because it gets cold at night. I'll make popcorn, but could someone bring drinks? We still {2} which movie to watch, so send me your ideas. Please arrive {3} 7:00. The movie starts at 7:30, and we don't want {4} the beginning!\nSofía","blanks":[{"o":["as long as","as soon as","even though","unless"],"why":"\"As long as\" means \"on the condition that\".","a":0},{"o":["didn't decide","don't decided","haven't decided","aren't decide"],"why":"\"Still\" + present perfect negative: the decision has not happened yet.","a":2},{"o":["until","since","during","before"],"why":"They should come before the movie starts.","a":3},{"o":["missing","to miss","miss","missed"],"why":"\"Want\" is followed by \"to\" + verb.","a":1}]},{"id":"F4G4","title":"Sports Day","kind":"Announcement","text":"Our annual Sports Day will take place next Friday on the school field. There will be no regular classes that day; {1}, every student will compete for one of four teams: Red, Blue, Green or Yellow. Your homeroom teacher {2} you which team you are on by Wednesday. Events include relay races, tug-of-war and a three-legged race, and there will also be games for students {3} prefer not to run. Please wear your team color and bring a hat and sunscreen. Water stations will be set up around the field, so there is no need {4} drinks from home. Parents are welcome {5} and cheer. At the end of the day, the team with the {6} points will receive the Sports Day Cup.","blanks":[{"o":["otherwise","although","instead","unless"],"why":"\"Instead\" means in place of regular classes.","a":2},{"o":["tells","told","has told","will tell"],"why":"\"By Wednesday\" is in the future: \"will tell\".","a":3},{"o":["who","which","whose","what"],"why":"\"Who\" refers to people (students) and is the subject of \"prefer\".","a":0},{"o":["bringing","to bring","bring","brought"],"why":"\"There is no need to do something\" uses the infinitive.","a":1},{"o":["to come","coming","come","came"],"why":"\"Be welcome to do something\" uses the infinitive.","a":0},{"o":["more","much","most","many"],"why":"\"The most points\" is a superlative: more than every other team.","a":2}]},{"id":"F4G5","title":"The Speech","kind":"Part of a short story","text":"Pablo hated speaking in front of people. Whenever a teacher asked him a question in class, his face {1} red and his voice disappeared. So when he was chosen to give the welcome speech at the school's anniversary, he wanted to hide. \"I can't do it,\" he told his older sister, Carolina. \"Everyone will laugh at me.\" Carolina thought for a moment. \"When I was your age, I was {2} nervous as you are,\" she said. \"Do you know what helped me? I practiced in front of our dog, Bruno, every night.\" Pablo laughed, but that evening he tried it. Bruno listened {3}, wagging his tail. The next night, Pablo practiced in front of Bruno and Carolina. Then in front of his whole family. Each time, his voice became a little stronger. On the day of the anniversary, Pablo walked onto the stage. Three hundred faces looked up at him. His hands were shaking, but he took a deep breath and imagined {4} talking to Bruno. When he finished, the audience clapped loudly. Afterward, the principal shook his hand. \"That's the best speech I {5} in years,\" she said. Pablo smiled. He still did not love public speaking, {6} he knew now that he could do it. And Bruno got an extra treat that night {7} his help.","blanks":[{"o":["turned","was turning to","has turned","turns into"],"why":"A repeated past action: past simple \"turned\".","a":0},{"o":["so","such","more","as"],"why":"\"As + adjective + as\" makes an equal comparison.","a":3},{"o":["patient","patiently","patience","patients"],"why":"An adverb describes how Bruno listened.","a":1},{"o":["he is","was he","he was","is he"],"why":"Reported thought in the past keeps statement word order: \"imagined he was\".","a":2},{"o":["have heard","hear","am hearing","was hearing"],"why":"\"In years\" (up to now) uses the present perfect.","a":0},{"o":["so","because","or","but"],"why":"\"But\" shows a contrast.","a":3},{"o":["by","for","with","from"],"why":"\"A treat for his help\" = as a reward for it.","a":1}]},{"id":"F4G6","title":"The World's Slowest Mammal","kind":"Magazine article","text":"Sloths are famous for being slow. They move so little that a sloth may stay in the same tree {1} several days. But being slow is not laziness; it is a clever way {2} survive. Sloths eat mostly leaves, which contain very {3} energy. To live on such a poor diet, a sloth's body works very slowly. Its stomach can take up to a month to digest a single meal! Because they move slowly and {4} make noise, sloths are hard for predators like eagles and jaguars to notice. Their fur helps them hide, too. Tiny green plants called algae grow in it, {5} the sloth the same color as the leaves around it. Sloths come down from their trees only about once a week, usually to go to the bathroom. This is the most dangerous moment of their week, {6} they cannot move quickly on the ground. Surprisingly, sloths are excellent swimmers. In water, they can move three times faster {7} on land. So the next time someone calls you a sloth, you can tell them that being slow {8} be a smart choice!","blanks":[{"o":["since","for","during","while"],"why":"\"For\" + a period of time (several days).","a":1},{"o":["for","of","to","by"],"why":"\"A way to do something\" uses the infinitive.","a":2},{"o":["few","less","a lot of","little"],"why":"\"Energy\" is uncountable, so we use \"little\". \"Few\" is for plural nouns.","a":3},{"o":["rarely","rare","rarer","rarity"],"why":"An adverb (\"rarely\") describes how often they make noise.","a":0},{"o":["makes","making","made","to making"],"why":"The -ing form shows the result of the algae growing: \"making the sloth green\".","a":1},{"o":["although","unless","if","because"],"why":"The second part gives the reason it is dangerous.","a":3},{"o":["then","as","than","that"],"why":"\"Faster than\" is a comparison.","a":2},{"o":["can","must to","should to","ought"],"why":"\"Can\" + base verb shows possibility. \"Must to\" and \"should to\" are not correct.","a":0}]}],"reading":[{"id":"F4R1","title":"Discovery Science Center: Visitor Information","kind":"Notice","level":"A2-B1","text":["Welcome to the Discovery Science Center! We are open Tuesday to Sunday from 9:00 a.m. to 6:00 p.m. We are closed on Mondays, except on public holidays.","Tickets: Students 12,000 pesos · Adults 18,000 pesos · Children under 5 free. School groups of 20 or more students receive a 25% discount if they book at least two weeks in advance.","Don't miss: the Earthquake Room, where you can feel what a real earthquake is like (minimum age 8); the Planet Dome, with shows every hour on the hour; and the Water Lab, where you can build your own dam. Please note that the Water Lab is closed for repairs until March 15.","Free audio guides are available in Spanish and English at the information desk. You will need to leave an ID card, which will be returned when you bring the audio guide back."],"questions":[{"type":"Detail","q":"When is the science center closed?","o":["On Sundays","On every public holiday","On Mondays that are not public holidays","Every weekend"],"why":"\"We are closed on Mondays, except on public holidays.\"","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"How can a school group get a discount?","o":["By booking at least two weeks before the visit","By visiting on a Monday","By bringing fewer than 20 students","By buying tickets at the information desk"],"why":"Groups of 20 or more get 25% off \"if they book at least two weeks in advance.\"","a":0},{"type":"Inference","q":"Who could NOT go into the Earthquake Room?","o":["A ten-year-old student","A six-year-old child","A teacher","A teenager"],"why":"The minimum age is 8.","a":1},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The phrase \"in advance\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["later","quickly","together","before"],"why":"Groups must book before the visit.","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"What must visitors do to get an audio guide?","o":["Leave an ID card at the information desk","Pay an extra fee","Speak English","Book it online"],"why":"\"You will need to leave an ID card.\" The guides are free.","a":0}]},{"id":"F4R2","title":"What the Attic Was Hiding","kind":"Narrative","level":"B1","text":["It rained for the whole first week of the vacation, and Natalia was bored. She had read all her books, finished every puzzle and watched more TV than her parents usually allowed. \"Why don't you help me clean the attic?\" her grandfather suggested. Natalia sighed, but she followed him up the narrow stairs.","The attic was dusty and dark, full of old furniture and boxes. In one corner, under a sheet, Natalia found a wooden box with a small lock. \"What's in here?\" she asked. Her grandfather's eyes widened. \"I thought I had lost that forever,\" he said quietly. He searched his pockets and, after a long time, found a tiny key on his key ring.","Inside the box were dozens of letters tied with a red ribbon, and an old black-and-white photograph of a young man and woman standing in front of a train. \"That's me,\" her grandfather said, \"and that's your grandmother, the day we met. I was a train conductor, and she was traveling to visit her sister in Santa Marta.\"","For the rest of the afternoon, they sat on the attic floor while he read the letters aloud. They were letters he and her grandmother had written to each other for two years before they got married. Natalia laughed at the funny parts and became quiet at the sad ones.","When the rain finally stopped, Natalia didn't even notice. \"Can we read more tomorrow?\" she asked. Her grandfather smiled. \"We can read them all,\" he said. \"Your grandmother would have liked that.\""],"questions":[{"type":"Detail","q":"Why was Natalia bored?","o":["Her grandfather would not play with her","It had rained all week and she had nothing left to do","She had no books","Her parents did not let her watch TV"],"why":"Paragraph 1: it rained all week and she had already read, done puzzles and watched TV.","a":1},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The phrase \"eyes widened\" in paragraph 2 means his eyes","o":["closed slowly","filled with tears","looked away","opened more in surprise"],"why":"He was surprised to see a box he thought he had lost.","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"How did the grandfather open the box?","o":["He broke the lock","Natalia found the key in the attic","With a tiny key on his key ring","The box was not locked"],"why":"He \"found a tiny key on his key ring.\"","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"Where did Natalia's grandparents first meet?","o":["On a train trip","At a wedding in Santa Marta","At her sister's house","At school"],"why":"He was a train conductor and she was traveling by train.","a":0},{"type":"Inference","q":"What can be inferred about Natalia's grandmother?","o":["She lives in Santa Marta","She is angry about the letters","She works as a train conductor","She is no longer alive"],"why":"\"Your grandmother would have liked that\" suggests she has died.","a":3},{"type":"Inference","q":"Why didn't Natalia notice that the rain had stopped?","o":["She was asleep","She was very interested in the letters","The attic had no windows","She was still bored"],"why":"She was so involved in the letters that she wanted to read more the next day.","a":1}]},{"id":"F4R3","title":"Colombia's Disappearing Glaciers","kind":"Academic (geography)","level":"B1-B2","text":["It may be surprising, but Colombia, a country close to the equator, has glaciers: large bodies of ice that stay frozen all year. They are found only on the highest mountains, around 4,800 meters above sea level or higher, where the air is cold enough for snow and ice to last.","Unfortunately, these glaciers are melting fast. In the middle of the 1800s, Colombia had fourteen glaciers. Today, only six remain, including those on the Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta, the Sierra Nevada del Cocuy and the Nevado del Ruiz. Scientists estimate that the country has lost about nine-tenths of its glacier ice since then, and some of the remaining glaciers could disappear in the next few decades.","The main cause is rising temperatures. As the climate warms, more ice melts each year than new snow can replace. Dark dust and ash, sometimes from volcanoes, can also cover the ice. Because dark surfaces absorb more sunlight than white ones, this makes the ice melt even faster.","Losing the glaciers is about more than losing beautiful views. Glaciers slowly release water into rivers and into the páramos, the high wetlands that supply drinking water to many Colombian towns and cities. Scientists are now carefully measuring the remaining glaciers, so that communities can plan for a future with less ice."],"questions":[{"type":"Main idea","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["Why Colombia's glaciers are shrinking and why that matters","How to climb Colombia's highest mountains","Why it snows near the equator","How volcanoes are formed"],"why":"The passage explains how fast the glaciers are melting, why, and what this means for water.","a":0},{"type":"Detail","q":"Where are Colombia's glaciers found?","o":["Near the coast","In the páramos","Only on the highest mountains","In every department"],"why":"Paragraph 1: they are found only on the highest mountains.","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"How many glaciers does Colombia have today?","o":["Fourteen","Nine","None","Six"],"why":"\"Today, only six remain.\"","a":3},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"replace\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["melt","take the place of","measure","cover"],"why":"New snow cannot make up for all the ice that melts.","a":1},{"type":"Detail","q":"Why does dark dust make the ice melt faster?","o":["The dust is hotter than the ice","The dust comes from volcanoes","Dark surfaces absorb more sunlight","The dust stops snow from falling"],"why":"\"Dark surfaces absorb more sunlight than white ones.\"","a":2},{"type":"Inference","q":"What could happen to some towns if the glaciers disappear?","o":["They could have less drinking water","They could get more snow","They could have more tourists","They could become colder"],"why":"Glaciers feed the rivers and páramos that supply drinking water.","a":0},{"type":"Purpose","q":"Why does the author mention the páramos?","o":["To describe a popular tourist place","To give an example of a glacier","To show where volcanoes are","To explain how glaciers help supply water to people"],"why":"The páramos carry glacier water to towns and cities.","a":3}]}]},{"id":5,"minutes":20,"listening":"F5","language":[{"id":"F5G1","title":"A Thank-You Letter","kind":"Letter","text":"Dear Grandma,\n\nThank you so much for the sweater you sent me for my birthday. It fits perfectly, and the color is my favorite! I {1} it every day since it arrived, because it has been so cold in Bogotá this month. Mom says you {2} it by hand last month. I can't believe it took you {3} three weeks! I'm saving some money from my birthday, and I hope {4} you in Pasto during the December vacation.\n\nWith love,\nAndrés","blanks":[{"o":["wear","am wearing","wore","have worn"],"why":"\"Since it arrived\" (up to now) uses the present perfect.","a":3},{"o":["have made","made","are making","make"],"why":"\"Last month\" is a finished time: past simple.","a":1},{"o":["almost","most","mostly","the most"],"why":"\"Almost\" means nearly: nearly three weeks.","a":0},{"o":["visiting","visit","to visit","visited"],"why":"\"Hope\" is followed by \"to\" + verb.","a":2}]},{"id":"F5G2","title":"Ciclovía Sundays","kind":"Notice","text":"Every Sunday and public holiday, from 7:00 a.m. to 2:00 p.m., Bogotá {1} more than 100 kilometers of its main roads to cars. During these hours, the streets belong to cyclists, runners, skaters and walkers. Anyone can join, and it is completely {2}. Please ride on the right side of the road and slow down {3} you pass people on foot. Children under twelve should ride with an adult. Along the route, you will find stations {4} you can put air in your tires or buy something to drink. Remember that the roads open to cars again at 2:00 p.m. sharp, {5} please leave the route before then.","blanks":[{"o":["close","closing","closes","is closed"],"why":"The subject \"Bogotá\" is singular and does the action: \"closes\".","a":2},{"o":["free","freely","freedom","freeing"],"why":"After \"is completely\" we need an adjective: \"free\".","a":0},{"o":["what","which","unless","when"],"why":"\"When\" means at the time that you pass them.","a":3},{"o":["which","where","who","when"],"why":"\"Where\" introduces information about a place (stations).","a":1},{"o":["so","but","because","although"],"why":"\"So\" shows the result: leave before 2:00.","a":0}]},{"id":"F5G3","title":"Lost Dog","kind":"Text message","text":"Hi Tomás, have you seen my dog, Kiwi? She ran out of the house this morning when the gardener {1} the gate open. She's small and brown with a red collar. She's very friendly but gets scared {2} she hears loud noises. If you see her, please don't chase her. Just call me, and I'll come right away. My mom and I {3} for her for two hours already, and we're getting really worried. Thanks, and please share this with anyone {4} lives near the park!\nValentina","blanks":[{"o":["leaves","left","has left","was leaving it"],"why":"A finished action this morning: past simple \"left\".","a":1},{"o":["wherever","whatever","however","whenever"],"why":"\"Whenever\" means every time that.","a":3},{"o":["are looking","looked","have been looking","were looked"],"why":"An action that started in the past and is still going on (\"for two hours already\").","a":2},{"o":["who","which","whose","what"],"why":"\"Who\" refers to people and is the subject of \"lives\".","a":0}]},{"id":"F5G4","title":"Charity Fun Run","kind":"Announcement","text":"On Saturday, May 18, our school will hold its first Charity Fun Run to raise money for the children's hospital in Chía. Students can choose to run or walk 3 or 5 kilometers around the park next to the school. To take part, you {1} sign up with your PE teacher by May 10. Each runner should ask family members and neighbors {2} a small amount of money for every kilometer they complete. You do not have to be fast {3} part; walking is perfectly fine! The race begins at 8:00 a.m., but please arrive by 7:30, so that we can give you your number. Every participant will receive a T-shirt, {4} was designed by students in the eighth-grade art class. All the money {5} will be used to buy books and games for the children's ward. Let's see {6} much we can raise together!","blanks":[{"o":["need to","needs to","need","are needing"],"why":"\"You need to\" + verb. \"Needs\" is for he/she/it.","a":0},{"o":["giving","give","to give","gave"],"why":"\"Ask someone to do something\" uses the infinitive.","a":2},{"o":["taking","to take","take","took"],"why":"\"To\" + verb shows purpose: in order to take part.","a":1},{"o":["who","what","where","which"],"why":"\", which\" adds information about a thing (the T-shirt).","a":3},{"o":["raised","raising","raises","to raise"],"why":"\"The money raised\" = the money that is raised (passive meaning).","a":0},{"o":["what","which","so","how"],"why":"\"How much\" asks about an amount.","a":3}]},{"id":"F5G5","title":"The Stray Dog","kind":"Part of a short story","text":"The dog appeared outside Camila's school on a cold Monday in June. It was thin and dirty, and one of its ears {1} torn. Every day after class, Camila shared part of her sandwich with it. She named it Lucky, although it did not seem very lucky. Her parents said she {2} keep it. \"We live in a small apartment,\" her father explained. \"A dog needs space to run.\" Camila understood, but she could not stop {3} about Lucky. One afternoon, she noticed a sign on the door of the animal clinic across the street: \"Volunteers needed to walk dogs.\" She went in and told the vet about Lucky. The vet, {4} name was Dr. Pardo, came outside with her and examined Lucky carefully. \"He's not sick, just hungry and tired,\" she said. \"We have a program that finds homes for dogs like him.\" Two weeks later, Lucky was adopted by an older man who lived on a farm {5} the city. He sent Camila a photo of Lucky running through a green field. Camila still walks dogs at the clinic every Saturday. \"Maybe I couldn't keep Lucky,\" she says, \"but I helped him find a better life than I {6} have given him. And now I help other dogs {7}.\"","blanks":[{"o":["were","has","is being","was"],"why":"\"One of its ears\" is singular, and the story is in the past.","a":3},{"o":["mustn't to","didn't can","couldn't","can't to"],"why":"Reported speech in the past: \"couldn't keep\". The other forms are not correct.","a":2},{"o":["thinking","to thinking","think","thought"],"why":"\"Stop doing something\" = no longer do it. She kept thinking about Lucky.","a":0},{"o":["who","whose","which","that"],"why":"\"Whose\" shows possession: the vet's name.","a":1},{"o":["out","outer","without","outside"],"why":"\"Outside the city\" = not in the city.","a":3},{"o":["can","could","will","may"],"why":"\"Could have given\" talks about something that was possible in the past but did not happen.","a":1},{"o":["as good","as much","as well","as far"],"why":"\"As well\" at the end of a sentence means \"too\".","a":2}]},{"id":"F5G6","title":"Why Do We Get Goosebumps?","kind":"Magazine article","text":"Have you ever listened to a beautiful song and felt the hair on your arms stand up? Those tiny bumps on your skin {1} goosebumps, because they look like the skin of a goose after its feathers have been removed. Goosebumps appear when tiny muscles at the base of each hair {2}, pulling the hair up. This usually happens when we are cold or {3} we feel strong emotions, like fear or excitement. Scientists believe goosebumps are a leftover from our ancient ancestors, {4} had much more hair than we do. When their hair stood up, it trapped a layer of warm air next to the skin, which helped them stay warm. It also made them look bigger, which could scare {5} enemies. You can see the same thing today when a frightened cat puffs up its fur. For humans, goosebumps are no longer very useful, {6} we do not have enough hair to keep warm this way. However, a recent study found that the same muscles may help hair grow, so goosebumps might not be completely useless {7} all. They are {8} example of how our bodies still carry clues to our past.","blanks":[{"o":["are called","call","are calling","have called"],"why":"The bumps receive the name: passive \"are called\".","a":0},{"o":["tightens","tighten","tightening","to tighten"],"why":"The subject \"muscles\" is plural, so the verb has no -s.","a":1},{"o":["what","which","where","when"],"why":"\"When\" introduces a time: when we feel strong emotions.","a":3},{"o":["which","whose","who","whom"],"why":"\"Who\" refers to people (our ancestors) and is the subject of \"had\".","a":2},{"o":["their","they","them","theirs"],"why":"A possessive adjective goes before a noun: \"their enemies\".","a":0},{"o":["although","unless","so that","because"],"why":"The second part gives the reason.","a":3},{"o":["at","after","in","for"],"why":"\"After all\" means in the end, despite what we thought.","a":1},{"o":["one many","more one","one more","many one"],"why":"\"One more example\" means an additional example.","a":2}]}],"reading":[{"id":"F5R1","title":"Photography Contest: My Neighborhood","kind":"Notice","level":"A2-B1","text":["Show us your neighborhood through your camera! This year's school photography contest is open to all students in grades 6 to 9. Photos can show people, places, animals or nature, as long as they were taken in the area where you live.","Rules: Each student may send a maximum of two photos. Photos can be taken with a phone or a camera, but they must not be changed with filters or editing apps. Every photo needs a title and one sentence explaining why you chose it. If a photo shows a person's face, you must have that person's permission.","How to enter: Email your photos to the art department by October 20. Write your name, your grade and the title of each photo in the email.","Prizes: The winner will receive a new camera, and the ten best photos will be shown in the school entrance during the parents' meeting in November."],"questions":[{"type":"Main idea","q":"What is the notice mainly about?","o":["A photography class","A trip around the neighborhood","A new camera store","A photography contest for students"],"why":"It gives the rules, how to enter and the prizes for a photo contest.","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"How many photos can each student send?","o":["One","Two","Three","Ten"],"why":"\"Each student may send a maximum of two photos.\"","a":1},{"type":"Detail","q":"What must a student do if a photo shows someone's face?","o":["Use a filter","Write the person's name","Get that person's permission","Send the photo to the person"],"why":"\"You must have that person's permission.\"","a":2},{"type":"Inference","q":"Which photo would NOT be accepted?","o":["A photo changed with a filter app","A photo taken with a phone","A photo of a dog in the student's street","A photo of a park near the student's house"],"why":"Photos \"must not be changed with filters or editing apps.\"","a":0},{"type":"Detail","q":"Where will the best photos be shown?","o":["In a museum","In the school entrance","On the school website","In a newspaper"],"why":"\"The ten best photos will be shown in the school entrance.\"","a":1}]},{"id":"F5R2","title":"Lost in the Fog","kind":"Narrative","level":"B1","text":["The hike to the top of the mountain was supposed to take three hours. Daniel, his older cousin Felipe and Felipe's friend Juan left early in the morning, when the sky was blue and the path was easy to see.","They reached the top just before noon and ate their sandwiches while looking at the valley below. But as they started walking back down, a thick fog rolled in. Within minutes, they could see only a few meters in front of them. The path, which had been so clear that morning, seemed to disappear.","\"Let's keep going,\" said Juan. \"I'm sure it's this way.\" But Daniel remembered something his scout leader had told him: if you get lost, stay where you are. \"Wait,\" he said. \"If we walk in the wrong direction, we'll get even more lost. We should stay together and wait for the fog to lift.\"","Felipe agreed. They sat close together on a large rock, put on their jackets and shared the last of their water. Daniel used his phone's compass to check which direction the town was in, and Felipe sent a message to his mother with their location, just in case.","An hour later, the fog cleared as quickly as it had appeared. They saw that the path was only twenty meters away, but Juan's direction would have taken them toward a steep cliff. \"I guess the youngest one was the smartest today,\" Felipe said, patting Daniel on the back."],"questions":[{"type":"Detail","q":"What was the weather like when the boys started the hike?","o":["Clear and sunny","Foggy","Rainy","Very windy"],"why":"\"The sky was blue and the path was easy to see.\"","a":0},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The phrase \"rolled in\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["went away","became lighter","arrived","turned into rain"],"why":"The fog came and covered the mountain.","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"What advice did Daniel remember?","o":["Always bring extra water","Follow the oldest person","Walk down quickly before dark","Stay where you are if you get lost"],"why":"\"If you get lost, stay where you are.\"","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"What did Felipe do while they waited?","o":["He used his phone's compass","He sent their location to his mother","He went to look for the path","He called the police"],"why":"\"Felipe sent a message to his mother with their location.\" Daniel used the compass.","a":1},{"type":"Inference","q":"What would probably have happened if they had followed Juan?","o":["They would have reached the town faster","They would have found the path","They could have been in danger near a cliff","They would have met other hikers"],"why":"\"Juan's direction would have taken them toward a steep cliff.\"","a":2},{"type":"Inference","q":"Why does Felipe call Daniel \"the smartest\"?","o":["His decision to wait kept them safe","He knew the path best","He had the best phone","He reached the top first"],"why":"Waiting instead of walking toward the cliff kept them safe.","a":0}]},{"id":"F5R3","title":"Tiny Birds, Huge Energy","kind":"Academic (science)","level":"B1-B2","text":["Hummingbirds are the smallest birds in the world. The bee hummingbird of Cuba weighs less than two grams, about the same as a small coin. Yet these tiny birds have some of the most amazing bodies in the animal kingdom. Colombia has more kinds of hummingbirds than almost any other country in the world.","To stay in the air in front of a flower, a hummingbird beats its wings up to eighty times per second. It is the only bird that can fly backward. All this movement requires a huge amount of energy. A hummingbird's heart can beat more than a thousand times a minute, and the bird must eat almost constantly, visiting hundreds of flowers every day to drink their sugary nectar.","This creates a problem at night. A hummingbird cannot eat while it sleeps, and without food, it could use up all its energy before morning. To survive, it enters a special state called torpor. Its body temperature drops by up to twenty degrees, and its heartbeat slows down dramatically. In torpor, the bird saves a large part of its energy. In the morning, it takes about twenty minutes to warm up again before it can fly.","Hummingbirds are also important for plants. As they drink nectar, pollen sticks to their heads and beaks, and they carry it to the next flower. Many flowers in the Andes are shaped like long tubes that only hummingbirds can reach, so these plants depend on them completely."],"questions":[{"type":"Main idea","q":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["Why Colombia has so many birds","The special features of hummingbirds and how they survive","How flowers make nectar","Where to see birds in Cuba"],"why":"The passage describes their size, energy, torpor and role in pollination.","a":1},{"type":"Detail","q":"What can hummingbirds do that no other bird can do?","o":["Drink nectar","Sleep at night","Visit many flowers","Fly backward"],"why":"\"It is the only bird that can fly backward.\"","a":3},{"type":"Detail","q":"Why must hummingbirds eat almost constantly?","o":["Their movement uses a huge amount of energy","They are very large birds","They store food for the winter","They must feed their young all day"],"why":"Paragraph 2: all this movement requires a huge amount of energy.","a":0},{"type":"Vocabulary","q":"The word \"dramatically\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["a little","slowly","a lot","loudly"],"why":"The heartbeat slows down very much.","a":2},{"type":"Detail","q":"What happens to a hummingbird in torpor?","o":["It flies to a warmer place","It drinks more nectar","It sleeps inside a flower","Its temperature and heartbeat go down"],"why":"\"Its body temperature drops ... and its heartbeat slows down.\"","a":3},{"type":"Reference","q":"The word \"it\" in paragraph 3 (\"it takes about twenty minutes to warm up\") refers to","o":["the morning","the bird","the energy","the heartbeat"],"why":"The bird needs about twenty minutes to warm up before it can fly.","a":1},{"type":"Inference","q":"What would probably happen to some Andean flowers if hummingbirds disappeared?","o":["They would grow shorter tubes at once","They would produce more nectar","Their pollen would not be carried to other flowers","They would change color"],"why":"\"These plants depend on them completely\" to carry pollen.","a":2}]}]}];
